# Shared declarations for every LIDAR canopy-height staging model.
#
# One `{code}_canopy_staging.preql` per city, each run by its own
# `canopy-{code}` [[cloud.job]] in ../trilogy.toml -- never by a city
# refresh.  What the table is and how it is built is the docstring of
# raw/shared/canopy.py; how a city refresh uses it is raw/tree_height.preql.
#
# Standalone like overture_staging/staging_common.preql: nothing here imports
# raw/, and nothing in raw/ imports this, which keeps a city refresh job from
# adopting a staging asset.  The concepts mirror raw/tree_height.preql's
# lookup columns *by name*: the staging parquet's column names are the
# contract the city model reads.
#
# The watermark is CANOPY_STAGED_THROUGH in raw/shared/canopy.py, a date set
# by hand: the surveys are one-off snapshots whose layers publish no edit
# date, so the job has no cron and is fired when that date is bumped.

key canopy_cell bigint; # one cell of the position grid, packed as lon_index * 1e8 + lat_index
property canopy_cell.city string;
property canopy_cell.canopy_crown_id string; # the survey's crown object, '{SURVEY}-{objectid}'
property canopy_cell.canopy_height_m float; # the crown's height, metres
property canopy_cell.canopy_crown_radius_m float; # the crown's radius, metres (clamped)
property canopy_cell.canopy_source string; # the survey, e.g. BOSTON_LIDAR_2024

property <*>.canopy_staged_through datetime; # the latest CANOPY_STAGED_THROUGH

# City-agnostic, like the Overture release probe: a freshness probe is not
# given the model's pushdown filter, so it reports the newest date across
# cities and bumping one city's date re-stages them all (a few minutes each).
root datasource canopy_staged (
    data_updated_through: canopy_staged_through
)
file `./canopy_staged_through.py`;
